      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We need to compute the sum of the floor values of the square roots of all integers from 1 to 16. The floor value of the square root of a number \\( n \\) is \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor = k\\) if \\( k^2 \\leq n < (k+1)^2 \\). We will determine the range for each \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{For } k = 1, \\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor = 1, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor = 1, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor = 2 \\\\\n&\\text{For } k = 2, \\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor = 2, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor = 2, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor = 2 \\\\\n&\\text{For } k = 3, \\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor = 3, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor = 3, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor = 3 \\\\\n&\\text{For } k = 4, \\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor = 3, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor = 3, \\quad \\ldots, \\quad \\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor = 4 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow, we will sum the floor values:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sum_{k=1}^{1} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 1 + 1 = 2, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k=2}^{3} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 2 + 2 = 4, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k=4}^{4} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 3 + 3 = 6, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k=5}^{5} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 3 + 3 = 6, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k=6}^{8} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 2 + 2 + 2 = 6, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k=9}^{12} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 3 + 3 + 3 = 9, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k=13}^{13} \\lfloor \\sqrt{k} \\rfloor = 3 = 3. \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding all these together, we get:\n\\[\n2 + 4 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 9 + 3 = 40.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
